{"packet_id": "8620d38f6a1a67c0", "dimensions": {"problem_framing": {"status": "scored", "score": 2, "event_ids": ["A1"], "reason": "A1 separates the objective (whether evidence justifies a consumer proposition) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives: extending the professional line, no expansion, and changing the route to market. It flags that current consumer reach is unknown. It does not correct a premise or surface a constraint a strong analyst would add, and the €20,000 / 12-week budget is an assumed constraint, not a sourced one."}, "causal_mechanism": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "Each theory names who benefits and a reason for behaviour change. The value-capture chain does not hold together. T1 (A9) markets an 'affordable alternative' but prices it 15% above professional models. Both A9 and A10 assume Tennant's existing professional distribution network reaches consumers, which nothing in the dossier supports. No coherent chain reaches value this manufacturer captures."}, "distinct_theories": {"status": "scored", "score": 2, "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "T1 (affordable convenience via e-commerce, A9) and T2 (premium professional-grade trust via retail, A10) are rival explanations with different positioning and channels. They converge in practice: both price above professional models (15% vs 20%) and target the same 'DIY consumers and small businesses'. This keeps them from implying clearly different products or customers."}, "assumptions_predictions": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "Assumptions and predictions are stated, but they are internally inconsistent and do not discriminate. A9 predicts sales at 15% above professional models yet also survey intent 'under $200'. A9 and A10 set unit thresholds (≥10 at +15%, ≥5 at +20%) that could both pass or both fail together, so neither theory is put in trouble relative to the other. The thresholds are unjustified assumptions."}, "evidence_judgement": {"status": "scored", "score": 0, "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14"], "reason": "Key sources are misread. A11 treats the 10-Q 'Sales direct to consumer' line as support for consumer adoption. In the excerpt it sits beside 'Sales to distributors', which indicates a direct-vs-distributor channel split, not household consumers. A12 cites the T360's 'economical price point ideal for tight customer budgets' as support for a premium theory, which runs against it. A14 relabels the Nilfisk verified_observation as a company_assertion and uses pressure-washer and vacuum growth as support for a premium professional-grade scrubber theory. The 'does not show' caveats are partly sensible but do not cure the misreadings. Date, place and customer-group fit are never addressed."}, "seeking_challenge": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A13", "A14"], "reason": "A13 runs one search against T1 but reports nothing about what it found. The next event (A14) instead assesses a returned source as supporting T2. No challenge to T2 is attempted. The contradictory reading of the T360 source (A12) is never recognised. This amounts to looking without engaging."}, "experiment_design": {"status": "not_reached", "score": null, "event_ids": ["A15"], "reason": "No test-design event occurred before the run stopped at A15. The prediction sketches in A9 and A10 are not a designed experiment, and their non-discriminating nature is flagged separately."}, "revision": {"status": "not_reached", "score": null, "event_ids": ["A15"], "reason": "A scheduled update was applicable but update_delivered is false, and the run stopped at A15 before any revision stage."}, "recommendation": {"status": "not_reached", "score": null, "event_ids": ["A15"], "reason": "A15: 'The investigation stopped before a recommendation (repeated errors).' No recommendation was produced."}}, "red_flags": [{"kind": "incomplete", "event_ids": ["A15"], "reason": "The episode stopped before a recommendation. Experiment design, revision and recommendation were never reached."}, {"kind": "misleading_quote", "event_ids": ["A11"], "reason": "A11 quotes 'Sales direct to consumer $ 212.3 ...' exactly but detaches it from the adjacent 'Sales to distributors' line. In that context it reads as a channel split for Tennant's existing sales, not evidence of household consumer demand. Presenting it as support for T1 misleads about its meaning."}, {"kind": "unsupported_inference", "event_ids": ["A12"], "reason": "The T360 source describes an 'economical price point ideal for tight customer budgets'. Citing it as support for a premium-priced, professional-grade consumer theory (T2) contradicts the source."}, {"kind": "unsupported_inference", "event_ids": ["A14"], "reason": "Nilfisk's consumer growth in pressure washers and vacuums in Western Europe does not support T2's claim that consumers will pay a premium for a professional-grade Tennant floor product. The source class is also mislabelled from verified_observation to company_assertion."}, {"kind": "unsupported_inference", "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "Both theories assert Tennant can leverage its existing distribution network to reach consumers. No dossier source supports this. T1 also contradicts itself by being 'affordable' yet priced above professional models."}, {"kind": "non_discriminating_test", "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "The prediction thresholds (≥10 units at +15% via e-commerce; ≥5 units at +20% via retail) can both succeed or both fail. They do not separate the convenience/affordability mechanism from the premium/trust mechanism."}, {"kind": "other", "event_ids": ["A5"], "reason": "A search result lists 'tennant-10q-customers', which does not appear among the supplied dossier sources. It is not relied on later."}], "suitable_for_training": false, "screen_reason": "The run is incomplete: it stopped at A15 with no recommendation, and revision was not reached despite a scheduled update. It also contains material unsupported inferences and a misleading quote: the 10-Q channel line read as consumer demand (A11), an economical-pricing source cited as support for a premium theory (A12), and a mismatched Nilfisk analogy (A14). Its predictions do not discriminate between the rivals (A9, A10)."}
